vements — with no indication of post-performance celebration or medal ceremony.\n\n✅ **4. Scott Moir present as her partner**  \n- Scott Moir is visible in every frame, dressed in black pants and a white shirt, skating in close coordination with Tessa.\n\n✅ **5. Olympic rings visible**  \n- The Olympic rings are clearly displayed on the boards behind the skaters in nearly every frame (e.g., Frame 1, 3, 5, 7, 12, 14, 15, 17, 18, 25, 26, 28, 29, 30).\n\n✅ **6. The specific type of performance being free dance (answer 3)**  \n- While the frames themselves do not explicitly label the segment as “free dance,” the context of the Vancouver 2010 Olympics, the attire, the length and complexity of the routine (as implied by the 30 frames of continuous performance), and the fact that this is the *only* full-length performance shown in this sequence strongly indicate this is the **free dance** portion — the final and most expressive segment of Olympic ice dance competition. In Vancouver 2010, Virtue and Moir famously won gold with their free dance to “Romeo and Juliet,” which matches the white costume and dramatic choreography seen here.\n\n---\n\n📌 **Conclusion**: All six criteria are satisfied. The performance depicted is the **free dance** at the **Vancouver 2010 Olympics**, performed by Tessa Virtue (in white dress) and Scott Moir, with Olympic branding and rings visible, and no indication of celebration — only active performance.\n\n✅ **Answer ‘3’ (Free dance) is correct.**'}, {'batch_start': 60, 'batch_end': 90, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2LH3JCGkEBU/frames/frame_0689.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2LH3JCGkEBU/frames/frame_0690.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2LH3JCGkEBU/frames/frame_0691.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2LH3JCGkEBU/frames/frame_0692.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2LH3JCGkEBU/frames/frame_0693.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2LH3JCGkEBU/frames/frame_0694.jp